- op: discuss  # 7fcb
  at: '2026-08-11T08:33:27Z'  # 7fcb
  by: agent  # 7fcb
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 7fcb
  clock: 4  # 7fcb
  nonce: 7fcb  # 7fcb
  message: "FIXED, and THE FIX THIS ITEM RECORDED WAS REFUTED BEFORE IT WAS BUILT.\n\nTHE ITEM'S PROPOSED FIX IS WRONG AT RUNTIME. The 2026-08-07 discussion named the\nfix site (tag_io_boundaries, correct) and the signal to use: 'meta[\"access_mode\"]\nis sitting right beside it and carries \"write\" for open(p, \"w\")'. That was\n\"verified by reading io_boundary.py:1793-1810\" -- i.e. the SITE was read, the VALUE\nwas not. Run against the live analyzer on a three-function fixture:\n\n    open(p)            -> access_mode='read'\n    open(p, \"w\")       -> access_mode='read'     <-- not 'write'\n    open(p, mode=\"a\")  -> access_mode='read'     <-- not 'write'\n\naccess_mode derives from the AST ROLE of the reference (ADR-0038's axiom), not from\nthe call's arguments. Consuming it would have classified EVERY open() as fs_read --\nconverting this item's false negative into a false negative of the same size, while\nappearing to fix it. A regression test (TestAccessModeIsNotTheSameFact) now pins\nthat the two keys encode different facts, so if a future ADR-0038 rebuild makes\naccess_mode argument-aware the test goes red and the keys get reconsidered.\n\nWHAT LANDED INSTEAD. The mode literal is read off the ast.Call at emission and\nrecorded as meta[\"io_mode\"]; one shared predicate (io_boundary.select_by_mode /\nresolve_mode_boundary) is consumed by BOTH the boundary tagger and the taint sink\nmatcher. Stamped ONCE at _process_call's single invocation rather than at its nine\nEdge.create sites, and keyed on the exact node rather than on a line number --\nADR-0038's retired classifier was line-granular and that is precisely why it\nmis-stamped the reads on an assignment's right-hand side as writes.\n\nA SECOND ROOT CAUSE THIS ITEM DID NOT NAME, and no mode information could have\nsurvived it: _rebuild_indices kept ONE row per qualified name (\"first one wins --\nshouldn't have duplicates\"), so the fs_write row for builtins.open was ABSENT from\nthe qualified index entirely. lookup_all now returns every row for a qualified name.\n\nTHE DEFECT RAN IN OPPOSITE DIRECTIONS IN THE TWO CONSUMERS, which is why both had\nto be fixed in one change. This item recorded the io-boundaries half (every open()\nread). The taint half is the mirror: the auto-sink derivation turns every fs_write\nrow into a host_fs sink regardless of mode, so every open() was a WRITE there --\n24 of the 35 distinct violations of the shipped runtime-cli-no-host-fs claim.\n\nMEASURED, hypergumbo's own tree, before -> after:\n  runtime-cli-no-host-fs   raw flows 1189 -> 370 ; distinct 35 -> 11\n                           open 24 -> 0 ; mkdir 10 -> 10 ; rmtree 1 -> 1\n  runtime-cli-no-subprocess (control, must not move)  distinct 33 -> 33\n  all 18 verdicts UNCHANGED -- host_fs stays correctly `violated` on the 11 real\n  unwrapped writes (INV-zudak, reopened), so precision rose without laundering a\n  capability, and the check-self-claims snapshot needs no rewrite.\n\nDENOMINATOR IS COMPLETE, NOT SAMPLED: _MAX_EVIDENCE_ROWS is 100 and head-truncates\na DEDUPLICATED list, so a 35-row evidence array is the whole distinct population.\n\nSCOPE DECIDED BY SORTING THE POPULATION BY WHAT THE FIX WOULD BE. 17 dual-classified\nprimitives exist across 6 languages, and only builtins.open + stdio.fopen are\nsettled by a mode literal. gen_udp.open (net_recv+net_send) and NSURLConnection.send*\ngenuinely cross two boundaries in one call; unistd.read/write are undecidable at the\ncall site; System.IO.hPrint x4 (fs_write+logging) is a taxonomy overlap;\nscala...Process.apply/run (fs_write+subprocess) is WI-bibuk's zone question.\nmode_discriminated_names admits ONLY the fs_read/fs_write pair and derives membership\nfrom the catalogue, with a parity test asserting the analyzer knows where each\nflagged name's mode argument sits.\n\nNOT DONE HERE: stdio.fopen (C) is discriminable by the same rule but C has no\nemitter recording the mode; the other 15 rows are separate shapes. Filed separately."  # 7fcb
